          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is the difference between flu and a cold?",
          "answer": "Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\n**Flu and a common cold are both viral infections of the nose, throat, and airways, but flu usually hits harder.**\n\nAccording to the \"Upper respiratory infection (common cold)\" reference, a cold usually starts gradually and is mostly nasal symptoms and sore throat. It rarely causes high fever or severe exhaustion in adults. According to the \"Influenza\" reference, flu usually starts suddenly, often within a few hours, with fever, chills, muscle aches, headache, marked fatigue, and dry cough. Both references say the difference is not reliable enough to tell for certain in one person.\n\nThe \"Upper respiratory infection (common cold)\" reference also says a cold is self-limiting in most otherwise healthy people. The \"Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship and when antibiotics are indicated\" guidance notes that colds often last about seven to ten days and that yellow or green nasal discharge is a normal part of a viral cold, so it does not by itself mean a bacterial infection.\n\nThe \"Influenza\" reference says flu is more likely to cause serious illness in older adults, young children, pregnant people, and people with long-term conditions. The \"Influenza: annual vaccination and the timing of antiviral treatment\" guidance recommends annual flu vaccination. It also says antibiotics have no effect on flu and are reserved for bacterial complications.\n\nThe retrieved passages did not cover when flu symptoms need urgent medical attention, so I can't tell you the warning signs from these sources. If you or someone you care about has flu-like symptoms and is in a high-risk group, or feels very unwell, speak with a clinician promptly. If breathing becomes difficult or someone seems seriously unwell, seek emergency care right away.",
